-\begin{abstract}
-Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) for historical law must retrieve connected authorities without treating overruled doctrine as current law. We introduce TERRA, an experimental GraphRAG pipeline combining an Event Evolution Graph over \CorpusTotalOpinions\ Supreme Court opinions, generic five-step procedural scaffolds, EASY/HARD routing and LLM-prompted evidence checking. Its scaffolds are not case-specific judicial reasoning. A historical \numUniqueQueries-query, five-system evaluation uses two non-Llama judges and paired tests with Benjamini--Hochberg correction. TERRA's Category~B faithfulness exceeds the retrieval baselines but remains below Direct LLM; its overall and answer-only scores describe different refusal and answered subsets. Adversarial safe handling is \TerraCatDSafeHandlingRate, with \TerraCatDFatalErrors\ fatal errors and false refusals on \TerraCatAFalseRefusals\ Category~A and \TerraCatBFalseRefusals\ Category~B queries. The obscure-precedent advantage depends on curated overruling annotations. A separate prospective comparison evaluates graph retrieval (T) against dense--BM25 retrieval (H) on the same 350 queries with shared evidence budgets, verification, generation and judges. On 100 Category~B pairs, mean evidence faithfulness is 0.76400 for T and 0.49515 for H (paired difference 0.26885; bootstrap 95\% CI [0.16035, 0.37205]; randomization $p\approx0.000010$). T triggers 69/100 verifier refusals versus H's 4/100: the gain concerns the gated pipeline, not demonstrated answer coverage or legal correctness. External and temporal evaluations remain mixed. Historical configuration gaps, annotation dependence and absent licensed-practitioner validation restrict the findings to an experimental research framework.
-\end{abstract}
